--TEST--
a bound listener, an accepted connection and a byte for byte round trip, all in one task — `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports` and `rule:security/net-listen-is-a-separate-grant-from-net-connect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Net\Listener::accept, Core\Net\Listener::port, Core\Net\Listener::close
// The whole of `Core\Net`'s TCP half, in the order a program meets it. One
// task and no deadlock, which is a property of TCP rather than of luck: a
// connect completes into the listener's backlog, so `connect` returns before
// anything has accepted and the `accept` after it finds a connection waiting.
var $door = Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 0);

// Port `0` asks the operating system to pick, and `port` is how the program
// learns which one it got — the member that makes an ephemeral bind usable.
// The number itself is different on every run and is never printed.
uint $port = $door->port();
echo "bound: ", ($port > 0) as string, "\n";

var $client = Core\Net::connect("127.0.0.1", $port, 5s);
var $served = $door->accept(5s);

// `write` answers what the socket took and `read` answers what arrived, so
// neither claims to have moved a whole buffer. Five octets over loopback is
// one of each.
echo "sent: ", $client->write("ping\n" as bytes, 5s) as string, "\n";
tainted bytes $heard = $served->read(64, 5s);
echo "heard: ", Core\Str::trim($heard as string), "\n";

echo "echoed: ", $served->write($heard, 5s) as string, "\n";
tainted bytes $back = $client->read(64, 5s);
echo "back: ", Core\Str::trim($back as string), "\n";

// Closing the client's half is what makes the server's next read the end of
// the stream, and the end of a stream is an empty answer rather than a throw:
// a program looping until there is nothing left has to be able to tell that
// apart from a failure.
$client->close();
tainted bytes $eof = $served->read(64, 5s);
echo "eof: ", (Core\Bytes::length($eof) == 0) as string, "\n";

// And closing the door leaves the connection that came through it alone: each
// accepted socket was filed under a key of its own.
$door->close();
echo "still served: ", $served->write("bye\n" as bytes, 5s) as string, "\n";
$served->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Three keys, because binding and dialling are separate powers and the loopback
# address the program supplies is one `rule:security/net-address-policy` denies
# by default. `listen = true` rather than an endpoint literal: the program asks
# for port `0`, so there is no endpoint a reviewer could write down in advance.
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
bound: 1
sent: 5
heard: ping
echoed: 5
back: ping
eof: 1
still served: 4
